        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A literary festival organiser speaking to volunteers about a children’s book festival\nMrs Helen: Right, everyone, if you could gather round the information desk. Thank you for giving up your evening to prepare for the Riverside Children’s Book and Storytelling Festival.\nFirst of all, let’s clarify the date. If you’ve seen the bookmarks we printed in January, they say Saturday thesecond of May. However, the town council is resurfacing the footbridge into the park that day, so we’ve moved the whole festival to Sunday the third. A few authors asked whether Bank Holiday Monday the fourth might attract a bigger crowd, but the youth brass band has booked the park bandstand for a concert on Monday, so Sunday the third is confirmed.\nNow, let’s talk about the budget for our ‘Books for Rural Schools’ charity fund. Last year we hoped to raise two thousand five hundred pounds, but we fell three hundred pounds short. Book-signing tent passes brought in twelve hundred pounds, which matched our forecast. Some of you suggested charging parents to enter the storytellingmeadow, but our arts council grant stipulates that all author readings must remain free. Nor can we charge authors a pitch fee, or they simply won’t come. What last year’s figures proved is that craft stalls linked to bookmaking are where families happily spend their loose change, so we’re counting on those stalls to make up the difference this year.\nSeveral of you will be helping in the Pop-Up Paper Engineering tent, where an illustrator teaches ten-year-olds how to make 3D pop-up castles. People assume children find it hard because the scissors are sharp, or because the instructions are in tiny print. Actually, we use pre-cut card templates and giant diagrams on a screen. The real stumbling block is that the card hinges must be creased sharply in both directions before gluing; if the children skip that step, the castle turret refuses to lie flat when the book is closed.\nNext door is our Bookmark Calligraphy and Wax-Sealing stall, which children absolutely adore. It’s fifty pence a go—the same price as the lucky dip—and there’s always a lively queue outside. Why is it such a magnet for young readers? Because even if a child smudges their ink lettering, our calligraphers stamp a golden wax owl emblem at the bottom and thread a silk tassel through the top, so nobody leaves without a treasure for their favourite book.\nLast year, that stall handed over one hundred and fifty pounds in net profit after we’d deducted fifty pounds for the sealing wax, card and silk tassels. Let’s make sure we have plenty of wax sticks ready for Sunday!",
