        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A railway heritage trust manager speaking to platform stewards about a steam locomotive centenary\nMr Whitaker: Afternoon, everyone. Step into the waiting room out of the wind. Thank you all for turning out for our briefing ahead of the Steam Locomotive Centenary event.\nFirst, let’s make sure everyone has the right date in their diary. Our early posters at the ticket office said Saturday theseventh—the seventh of June. However, the boiler inspector required an extra forty-eight hours to pressure-test the locomotive’s new copper tubes, so we’ve moved the main celebration to Saturday the fourteenth. A couple of trustees wondered about Sunday the fifteenth, but Network Rail is shutting the branch line junction for track ballasting that Sunday, so Saturday the fourteenth is our only window.\nNow, onto the restoration fund for the vintage dining carriage. Last year we aimed to raise five thousand pounds and fell seven hundred pounds short. Steam-train ride tickets brought in three thousand two hundred pounds, which was capacity—every single seat was sold. Because the carriages are already full, we can’t sell any more train tickets, and raising fares would price out local pensioners. Nor will we charge admission to the station platform itself. That means our platform side-stalls and heritage experiences must bring in that extra seven hundred pounds this year.\nTwo of you will be managing the Victorian Signal-Box Simulator at the north end of Platform One. Visitors pull the heavy steel levers in the right sequence to clear a train through the junction. People often think novices fail because the steel levers require huge physical strength, or because the bell codes are too fast to count. Actually, we’ve disconnected the heavy counterweights, and the bell rings slowly. The real catch is the mechanical interlocking frame underneath the floor: if a player forgets to return the distant signal lever to ‘danger’ first, all the other levers lock solid and won’t budge an inch.\nFurther down the platform is the Brass Ticket-Stamping Booth, which is a massive hit with children and grandchildren. At two pounds fifty a ticket, it’s not cheap, and there’s always a line outside the booth. What makes it so popular is that, using our 1890s date-press, every child gets to punch the exact date and their own birth year into a solid brass souvenir platform ticket, which we then clip onto a keyring for their schoolbag.\nLast year, that booth contributed three hundred and twenty pounds in clear profit after we’d deducted eighty pounds for the blank brass plates and keyrings. Right, let’s walk the platform!",
