        "tapescript": "PART 1 – (Questions 1–5) A community coordinator speaking to volunteers about a quarterly Repair Café\nMs Sonia: Morning, everyone! Grab a name badge and gather round the workbench. Thanks so much for giving up your weekend for our quarterly Town Hall Repair Café.\nFirst, a quick reminder about the date change. The town council newsletter printed Saturday the eighth of March, which was our usual second-Saturday slot. However, the blood-transfusion service needed the main hall for an emergency donor session that day, so we’ve shifted the Repair Café to Saturday the fifteenth. A couple of our electricians asked if Sunday the sixteenth was free, but the caretaker locks the municipal building on Sundays to save on heating, so Saturday the fifteenth is our confirmed date.\nNow, let’s talk about funding our shared tool library. Last quarter we aimed to raise six hundred pounds for replacement soldering irons and sewing machines, and we finished one hundred andfifty—one hundred and fifty pounds short. The tea and homemade cake counter brought in two hundred and eighty pounds, which was fantastic. Now, some of you suggested charging residents a five-pound booking fee to have their toaster or bicycle examined, but the whole ethos of the Repair Café movement is that repairs are carried out free of charge. Nor can we skimp on safety testing plugs. That means our side-workshops and spare-parts stalls inside the hall must bring in that extra money this time.\nFor those of you hosting the Vintage Clockwork table, a quick heads-up. Residents bring in old wind-up mantel clocks that have stopped ticking. People assume the main problem is that the mainspring has snapped, or that brass gears have worn down over the decades. In fact, nineteenth-century brass movements are almost indestructible. What actually makes these repairs so delicate is that previous owners have usually sprayed modern silicone lubricant inside the case, which turns into a sticky gum that glues the tiny escapement pins together.\nRight next to the café counter, we’re running the 3D-Printed Spare Parts stall, which is hugely popular with teenagers and keen DIYers. At one pound twenty per print, it’s very reasonable, though each print takes about twelve minutes. Why do young people crowd around it? Because even if we don’t have a broken battery cover for them to fix, our tech volunteers let every participant scan and print a custom cable-tidy clip in their favourite neon colour, so nobody leaves empty-handed.\nLast quarter, that 3D printer made a net profit of one hundred and forty pounds after we’d taken off forty pounds for the biodegradable plastic filament spools. Let’s switch on the soldering irons and get ready!",
