        "tapescript": "PART 2 – (Questions 6–10) A radio report about an urban cooling system called AeroMoss\nPresenter: Welcome back to ‘Future Cities’. With European summers growing hotter, architects are looking beyond air conditioning to keep urban streets bearable. Today we examine AeroMoss, a modular wall system developed by the Swiss start-up Helvetia GreenTech that has just completed a twelve-month pilot in Zurich.\nThe project’s lead architect, Dr Lukas Meyer, says people often misunderstand what the moss panels are meant to do.\nDr Lukas Meyer: “Passers-by admire the velvety green texture, and acoustic tests show the panels do muffle a little street traffic noise. But aesthetics and soundproofing were merely welcome bonuses, and we certainly aren’t competing with rooftop solar panels, since moss generates no electricity. Our single overriding objective was to pull down pavement temperatures in dense city canyons by up to four degrees without consuming kilowatts of grid power.”\nPresenter: Unlike conventional vertical gardens, which require soil and constant pumping, AeroMoss relies on hardy alpine moss species. Under normal conditions, the panels harvest morning dew and recycled greywater from the building’s sinks. However, Zurich’s sensors monitor the air continuously, and whenever relative humidity stays below thirty percent for three days in a row, an automated valve opens a reserve rainwater tank in the basement so the moss never dries out.\nThe secret behind the system’s durability lies right behind the moss: a thin, honeycomb ceramic plate. Moss has no true roots, and on bare concrete it would peel off during winter freezes. The ceramic backing holds moisture evenly across the surface and insulates the moss fibres so they survive sub-zero temperatures intact.\nIntriguingly, that ceramic layer came from an unlikely partner. A venerable German manufacturer approached Helvetia GreenTech after reading about the prototype. The German firm knew nothing about horticulture or electronic weather sensors, nor does it make water pumps. For decades, however, it has produced ultra-porous clay membranes for industrial chemical filters—which turned out to be the exact material needed to hold water for the moss.\nSo, will we soon see AeroMoss cladding office blocks across Europe? Dr Meyer remains realistic.\nDr Lukas Meyer: “City mayors love the headline figures, but municipal budgets are stretched thin. Until our second-year audit proves that annual upkeep costs less than pruning and watering traditional street trees, most councils will wait on the sidelines.”",
